<p>The UK is bringing together representatives from about 35 countries on Thursday to discuss how to reopen the Strait of Hormuz, after the waterway was effectively shut by the Middle East war and Iran&#x27;s response to the US-Israeli war on Iran. Prime Minister Keir Starmer said the talks will look at diplomatic and political options to restore freedom of navigation, protect ships and seafarers, and restart the flow of vital commodities through one of the world&#x27;s most important energy corridors. The meeting reflects a broad multilateral push. Countries including France, Germany, Italy, Japan, Australia, South Korea, Canada, and the UAE are expected to take part, while the United States is not included in the summit described by the sources. Starmer said reopening the strait &quot;will not be easy,&quot; but the initiative shows a coordinated effort to prepare for safer passage after the fighting stops, at a time when disruption in Hormuz has already pushed up global oil and gas prices.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/7_8YCmXqkVoW3LpOvuoXo8hnLdMEVYympV8S3EwYudFfStVoz5BkkYN845o7Sv6erHO3YMlWCpTpPyBv9oQEMuTs_EhGIfqr-tAr53UCLIj-' alt='Britain’s Prime Minister Keir Starmer speaks during a press conference at Downing Street in London, Britain, April 1, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Next phase: Starmer said Britain will also bring together military planners after the diplomatic meeting to consider how to marshal capabilities and make the strait accessible and safe once fighting has stopped.</li><li>Australian role: Australian Foreign Minister Penny Wong is set to represent Australia at the summit, underscoring Canberra&#x27;s support for a diplomatic path to restoring shipping access.</li><li>Virtual format: A British official said Yvette Cooper will host the discussions as a virtual meeting on Thursday, with an initial focus on diplomatic and political measures.</li><li>Early planning: A British official said any initial operational phase will likely center on mine-hunting before broader steps to secure passage are considered.</li></ul><blockquote>I do have to level with people on this. This (reopening) will not be easy. - Keir Starmer</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>UK government: Prime Minister Keir Starmer said the group will assess diplomatic and political options to restore navigation, protect trapped ships and seafarers, and resume commodity flows through the strait. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/uk-gathers-30-countries-plot-ways-reopening-strait-131640331'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>Australia: Foreign Minister Penny Wong&#x27;s planned attendance signals Australia&#x27;s backing for the UK-led diplomatic effort rather than a unilateral approach to the shipping crisis. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/apr/02/penny-wong-to-join-talks-with-35-countries-excluding-us-to-explore-ways-to-reopen-strait-of-hormuz'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>British officials: A British official said post-conflict access planning may start with mine-hunting, suggesting officials expect technical maritime clearance to be a necessary first step. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1987674/uk-to-host-35-state-moot-on-reopening-of-hormuz-strait'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/uk-gathers-30-countries-plot-ways-reopening-strait-131640331'>UK gathers more than 30 countries to plot ways of reopening the Strait of Hormuz</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/apr/02/penny-wong-to-join-talks-with-35-countries-excluding-us-to-explore-ways-to-reopen-strait-of-hormuz'>Penny Wong to join talks with 35 countries, excluding US, to explore ways to reopen strait of Hormuz</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1987674/uk-to-host-35-state-moot-on-reopening-of-hormuz-strait'>UK to host 35-state  moot on reopening of Hormuz Strait</a> - dawn.com</li></ul>